course: world
lesson: 71
title: Rockets, satellites and astronauts
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A rocket works like a balloon let go: it throws hot gas out of its tail very hard, and the gas pushes the rocket the other way. It needs no air to push against, so it works in empty space too.
explain: Fuel needs oxygen to burn, and there is no air in space, so a rocket carries its own oxygen as well as its fuel. Big rockets are built in stages, and each empty stage drops away to make the rocket lighter.
explain: A satellite stays up because it moves sideways very fast, about 8 kilometres every second; it keeps falling towards the Earth, but the curved ground falls away beneath it just as fast, so it falls around the Earth forever. This path is called an orbit.
explain: Satellites forecast the weather, carry phone calls and television, help us find our way and photograph the land; the International Space Station is a large satellite where astronauts live and work about 400 kilometres up.
explain: For example, the space station goes around the Earth about every 90 minutes, so the astronauts on board see about 16 sunrises and 16 sunsets every day.
fact: A rocket pushes hot gas backwards, and the gas pushes the rocket forwards.
fact: A rocket carries its own oxygen because there is no air in space.
fact: A jet plane's engines burn their fuel with oxygen from the air around them, so a plane cannot fly in space.
fact: Big rockets drop their empty stages to become lighter.
fact: A satellite in orbit is falling around the Earth while it moves sideways very fast.
fact: The first satellite, Sputnik 1, was launched in 1957.
fact: Yuri Gagarin became the first person in space in 1961.
fact: Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin walked on the Moon in 1969.
fact: The International Space Station circles the Earth about every 90 minutes, about 400 kilometres up.
fact: Satellites are used for weather forecasts, phone calls, television, maps and finding the way.
fact: A satellite about 36,000 kilometres up goes around once a day and stays above the same spot on the Earth.
fact: A spacesuit gives an astronaut air, the right pressure and protection from heat and cold.
fact: Astronauts' muscles and bones weaken in space, so they exercise about two hours every day.
exercise: How does a rocket move forward? | It pushes hot gas backwards and the gas pushes it forwards | It pushes against the air in front of it | It is pulled up by the Sun | It floats up because its fuel is lighter than the air around it
exercise: Why does a rocket carry its own oxygen? | There is no air in space for the fuel to burn with | The astronauts need all of it to breathe during the trip | Oxygen makes the whole rocket much lighter as it climbs | Oxygen keeps the rocket's engines cool so they do not melt
exercise: Why do big rockets drop their empty stages? | To make the rocket lighter | To slow the rocket down | To steer it towards the Moon | To leave fuel for the next trip
exercise: Why does a satellite not fall down to the Earth? | It moves sideways so fast that it keeps falling around the Earth | There is no gravity where it flies | Its engines push it up all the time | It is held up by the thin air
exercise: What was the first satellite? | Sputnik 1, in 1957 | The Moon landing craft, in 1969 | The space station, in 1998 | A weather balloon, in 1900
exercise: Who was the first person in space? | Yuri Gagarin | Neil Armstrong | Isaac Newton | Christopher Columbus
exercise: Who were the first people to walk on the Moon? | Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin | Yuri Gagarin and Valentina Tereshkova | The Wright brothers | Galileo and Newton
exercise: How long does the International Space Station take to go around the Earth? | About 90 minutes | About one whole day | About one month or so | About one year, like the Earth
exercise: About how high does the International Space Station fly? | About 400 kilometres | About 10 kilometres | About 40,000 kilometres | About 400,000 kilometres
exercise: What do satellites help us do? | Forecast the weather, call people and find our way | Make it rain over dry farms whenever the farmers need it | Keep the Moon from falling | Push clouds across the sky
exercise: What does a spacesuit give an astronaut? | Air, the right pressure and protection from heat and cold | Extra gravity to stop floating | The power to fly without a ship | Food that never runs out
exercise: Why do astronauts exercise for hours every day? | Their muscles and bones weaken in space | They must keep warm in the cold station | They have nothing else to do | Exercise makes the station fly faster
exercise: How fast does a satellite near the Earth travel? | About 8 kilometres every second | About as fast as a car on a motorway | About as fast as a passenger plane | It does not move at all
exercise: Why does a television satellite seem to hang still above one spot? | It goes around once a day, just as the Earth turns | It has stopped moving in space | It is tied to a tall tower | The Earth stops turning beneath it while it stays still
test: A balloon is blown up and let go, and it shoots across the room. How is this like a rocket? | Both are pushed forward by gas rushing out of the back | Both are lifted because they are lighter than the air around them | Both are pulled along by the wind | Both burn fuel to fly
test: If a satellite slowed down a lot, what would happen? | It would fall lower and could drop into the air and burn up | It would fly further and further away from the Earth into space | It would stay in exactly the same place up in the sky forever | It would start to go faster and faster by itself, all on its own
test: Why could a jet plane not fly to the Moon? | Its engines need air, and space has none | Planes are too small to see the Moon | The Moon is too close for a plane | Planes cannot fly at night, when the Moon is out
test: An astronaut on the space station says she saw the Sun rise many times today. Why? | The station goes around the Earth every 90 minutes | The Sun moves faster near space | She was looking at different stars | Clouds kept hiding and then showing the Sun again and again
test: A sailor in the middle of the ocean knows exactly where his ship is. What most likely helps him? | Signals from navigation satellites | The colour of the sea under the ship | The direction of the waves around them | The shape of the clouds in the sky above
test: Which order of events is right? | The first satellite, then the first person in space, then the Moon walk | The Moon walk, then the first satellite, then the first person in space | The first person in space, then the Moon walk, then the first satellite | The first satellite, then the Moon walk, then the first person in space
test: An astronaut comes home after six months in space. Why might she need help to walk at first? | Her muscles and bones grew weaker in space | She forgot how to walk | The Earth's gravity grew stronger while she was away | Her spacesuit is still too heavy
test: A ball thrown sideways from a very high tower lands far away. If it could be thrown fast enough, with no air in the way, what would happen? | It would fall all the way around the Earth | It would fly straight up into space | It would stop in mid-air | It would land right at the foot of the tower
test: Why is a rocket launched with an enormous amount of fuel? | It must lift its own heavy weight and reach a huge speed | Fuel keeps the astronauts warm | The fuel is used to make air on the Moon | Rockets are made heavier on purpose
test: why do astronauts on the space station gotta work out every day | Muscles and bones weaken in space, so they exercise about two hours a day | There is too much food on board, so they need to burn it off | Exercise keeps the station spinning the right way | It keeps them warm, since space is very cold
test: who went up into space first | Yuri Gagarin, in 1961 | Neil Armstrong, in 1969 | Buzz Aldrin, in 1957 | Sputnik's pilot, in 1957
test: Leo says satellites stay up cos there's no gravity up there. true? | No, a satellite is falling around the Earth while moving sideways fast | Yes, gravity stops a little way above the clouds | Yes, but only above the Moon | No, satellites stay up because they hang from strong cables
test: my brother reckons rockets push on the air to fly. right? | No, the rocket pushes hot gas backwards and the gas pushes it forwards | Yes, the rocket pushes off the air below it | Yes, so rockets cannot fly at all once they reach space where there is no air | No, rockets are pulled up by the Sun
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the propulsion of a rocket? | It pushes hot gas backwards, and the gas pushes the rocket forwards | It pushes against the air beneath it, which lifts it upwards | It is drawn upwards by the gravity of the Moon | It is lifted by wings as an aeroplane is lifted
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a rocket carries its own oxygen. | There is no air in space | Oxygen renders the rocket lighter | Oxygen protects the rocket from heat | Oxygen is required for the astronauts' food alone
exam: Ascertain the person who became the first in space, together with the year thereof. | Yuri Gagarin, in 1961 | Neil Armstrong, in 1969 | Buzz Aldrin, in 1957 | Yuri Gagarin, in 1957
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning a satellite in orbit. | It falls around the Earth whilst moving sideways very fast | It floats because there is no gravity in space | It is held aloft by its engines continuously | It rests upon the top of the atmosphere
